export const BAA_V1_0_TEXT = "# Business Associate Agreement\n\n_Version 1.0. Effective October 12, 2026._\n\nThis Business Associate Agreement (\"Agreement\") is entered into between the clinic or pharmacy that accepts it in the CompoundIQ onboarding portal, as identified by the legal name it provides there (\"Covered Entity\"), and CompoundIQ (\"Business Associate\"), each a \"Party\", and is effective on the date it is accepted in the CompoundIQ onboarding portal (the \"Effective Date\").\n\n## 1. Definitions\n\nCapitalized terms used but not defined in this Agreement have the meanings given in the HIPAA Rules, including: Breach, Data Aggregation, Designated Record Set, Disclosure, Health Care Operations, Individual, Minimum Necessary, Notice of Privacy Practices, Protected Health Information (\"PHI\"), Electronic Protected Health Information (\"ePHI\"), Required by Law, Secretary, Security Incident, Subcontractor, Unsecured Protected Health Information, and Use.\n\n\"HIPAA Rules\" means the Privacy, Security, Breach Notification, and Enforcement Rules at 45 CFR Part 160 and Part 164, as amended, including by the HITECH Act.\n\n\"Services\" means the CompoundIQ platform services described in the CompoundIQ Terms of Service accepted by Covered Entity, including electronic prescription ordering for compounded medications, transmission of prescriptions to licensed pharmacies, patient payment collection, order status tracking, and related support.\n\n## 2. Obligations and Activities of Business Associate\n\nBusiness Associate agrees to:\n\n- Not Use or Disclose PHI other than as permitted or required by this Agreement or as Required by Law.\n- Use appropriate safeguards, and comply with Subpart C of 45 CFR Part 164 with respect to ePHI, to prevent Use or Disclosure of PHI other than as provided for by this Agreement.\n- Report to Covered Entity any Use or Disclosure of PHI not provided for by this Agreement of which it becomes aware, including Breaches of Unsecured PHI as required by 45 CFR 164.410, and any Security Incident of which it becomes aware, without unreasonable delay and in no case later than ten (10) calendar days after discovery. The Parties agree that this paragraph is notice of the ongoing existence of unsuccessful Security Incidents (such as pings, port scans, and blocked log-in attempts) for which no further notice is required.\n- In accordance with 45 CFR 164.502(e)(1)(ii) and 164.308(b)(2), ensure that any Subcontractors that create, receive, maintain, or transmit PHI on behalf of Business Associate agree in writing to the same restrictions, conditions, and requirements that apply to Business Associate with respect to such information.\n- Make available PHI in a Designated Record Set to Covered Entity as necessary to satisfy Covered Entity's obligations under 45 CFR 164.524, within ten (10) days of a request.\n- Make any amendment to PHI in a Designated Record Set as directed or agreed to by Covered Entity under 45 CFR 164.526, or take other measures as necessary to satisfy Covered Entity's obligations under 45 CFR 164.526.\n- Maintain and make available the information required to provide an accounting of Disclosures to Covered Entity as necessary to satisfy Covered Entity's obligations under 45 CFR 164.528.\n- To the extent Business Associate carries out an obligation of Covered Entity under Subpart E of 45 CFR Part 164, comply with the requirements of Subpart E that apply to Covered Entity in the performance of that obligation.\n- Make its internal practices, books, and records available to the Secretary for purposes of determining compliance with the HIPAA Rules.\n- Limit its Uses and Disclosures of, and requests for, PHI to the Minimum Necessary to accomplish the intended purpose.\n\n## 3. Permitted Uses and Disclosures by Business Associate\n\n- Business Associate may Use or Disclose PHI as necessary to perform the Services, including Disclosure of prescription and shipping information to the licensed pharmacy selected for an order, and Disclosure to payment processors only to the extent necessary to collect payment.\n- Business Associate may Use or Disclose PHI as Required by Law.\n- Business Associate may not Use or Disclose PHI in a manner that would violate Subpart E of 45 CFR Part 164 if done by Covered Entity, except for the specific Uses and Disclosures set out below.\n- Business Associate may Use PHI for its proper management and administration or to carry out its legal responsibilities, and may Disclose PHI for those purposes if the Disclosure is Required by Law, or if Business Associate obtains reasonable assurances from the recipient that the information will remain confidential, will be Used or further Disclosed only as Required by Law or for the purposes for which it was Disclosed, and that the recipient will notify Business Associate of any instance of which it is aware in which the confidentiality of the information has been breached.\n- Business Associate may provide Data Aggregation services relating to the Health Care Operations of Covered Entity.\n- Business Associate may de-identify PHI in accordance with 45 CFR 164.514(a) through (c). Business Associate may use de-identified information to operate, secure and improve the Services, and will not sell it or attempt to re-identify it.\n- Business Associate will not sell PHI and will not Use or Disclose PHI for marketing, except as permitted by the HIPAA Rules.\n\n## 4. Obligations of Covered Entity\n\n- Covered Entity shall notify Business Associate of any limitation in its Notice of Privacy Practices, any change in or revocation of an Individual's permission, and any restriction on Use or Disclosure that Covered Entity has agreed to under 45 CFR 164.522, to the extent it may affect Business Associate's Use or Disclosure of PHI.\n- Covered Entity shall not request Business Associate to Use or Disclose PHI in any manner that would not be permissible under Subpart E of 45 CFR Part 164 if done by Covered Entity.\n- Covered Entity is responsible for obtaining any patient consents its jurisdiction requires, including consent to receive text messages, before entering a patient's contact information in the Services.\n\n## 5. Term and Termination\n\n- Term. This Agreement is effective on the Effective Date and continues until the Terms of Service between the Parties end, unless terminated earlier under this section.\n- Termination for cause. Either Party may terminate this Agreement if the other Party has violated a material term and has not cured the violation within thirty (30) days of written notice.\n- Obligations on termination. On termination, Business Associate shall return to Covered Entity or destroy all PHI received from, or created or received by Business Associate on behalf of, Covered Entity that Business Associate still maintains in any form, and retain no copies. Where return or destruction is not feasible, including where records must be retained under applicable pharmacy, prescription, or payment record-keeping laws, Business Associate shall extend the protections of this Agreement to that PHI and limit further Uses and Disclosures to those purposes that make return or destruction infeasible, for as long as Business Associate maintains it. Records that the HIPAA Rules require to be retained are kept for at least six (6) years.\n- Survival. The obligations of Business Associate under this section survive termination of this Agreement.\n\n## 6. Miscellaneous\n\n- Regulatory references. A reference in this Agreement to a section of the HIPAA Rules means the section as in effect or as amended.\n- Amendment. The Parties agree to amend this Agreement as necessary for compliance with the HIPAA Rules and any other applicable law.\n- Interpretation. Any ambiguity in this Agreement shall be interpreted to permit compliance with the HIPAA Rules.\n- No third-party beneficiaries. Nothing in this Agreement confers any rights on any person other than the Parties.\n- Governing law. This Agreement is governed by the HIPAA Rules and, to the extent not preempted, the laws of the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania.\n- Electronic acceptance. Acceptance of this Agreement in the CompoundIQ onboarding portal by an authorized representative of Covered Entity, recorded with the representative's name, title, account, date and time, and the version and text of this Agreement, constitutes execution by Covered Entity.\n"

/** src/content/legal/clinic-terms-v1.0.md (sha256 02e37bb458f5ba008e27b669440f186f1a312a20201b7152e424a40a050410a5) */
export const CLINIC_TERMS_V1_0_TEXT = "# CompoundIQ Clinic Terms of Service\n\n_Version 1.0. Effective October 12, 2026._\n\nThese Clinic Terms of Service (the \"Terms\") are an agreement between CompoundIQ (\"CompoundIQ\", \"we\") and the medical practice that accepts them in the CompoundIQ onboarding portal (\"Clinic\", \"you\"). They take effect when CompoundIQ approves your clinic in the portal (the \"Effective Date\"). The Business Associate Agreement you accept in the portal (the \"BAA\") is part of these Terms.\n\n## 1. Definitions\n\n- \"Platform\" means CompoundIQ's software, portals, order routing, payment collection and related services.\n- \"Provider\" means a licensed practitioner at your Clinic who prescribes on the Platform. \"Staff\" means the medical assistants, administrators and other people you invite.\n- \"Pharmacy\" means an independent licensed pharmacy that receives Orders through the Platform.\n- \"Order\" means a signed, patient-specific prescription, with the patient, shipping and payment information needed to fill it, sent to a Pharmacy through the Platform.\n- \"Applicable Law\" means all federal, state and local laws, regulations and board rules that apply to your activities, including medical practice acts, prescribing rules, telehealth rules, the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act, the Controlled Substances Act, HIPAA, consumer protection and text-messaging laws, the federal Anti-Kickback Statute and state anti-kickback, fee-splitting, corporate practice and patient-brokering laws.\n\n## 2. What CompoundIQ does and does not do\n\n- CompoundIQ provides technology that lets your Providers create and sign prescriptions for compounded and other medications, routes each Order to the Pharmacy your Provider selects, collects payment from the patient, and tracks the Order to delivery.\n- CompoundIQ is not a medical practice, pharmacy, insurer or payer. CompoundIQ does not practice medicine or pharmacy, does not give medical advice, and does not direct or control your Providers' clinical judgment.\n- CompoundIQ does not choose the Pharmacy for a patient. Your Providers and patients choose, and CompoundIQ does not steer Orders to any Pharmacy in return for anything of value.\n\n## 3. Accounts and security\n\n- Your clinic administrator is responsible for the Providers and Staff you invite, for giving each person only the access their role needs, and for removing access promptly when someone leaves.\n- Each person must use their own account and keep their credentials and two-step sign-in secure. You are responsible for activity under your accounts and will tell CompoundIQ promptly if you suspect unauthorized access.\n- The information you give in onboarding, including your practice details, Providers, licenses and agreements, must be accurate and kept current.\n\n## 4. Providers, licensure and prescribing\n\n- Only Providers who hold an active license, and any registration Applicable Law requires, in the state where the patient is located may sign prescriptions for that patient. You will keep each Provider's NPI and state licenses current on the Platform and will notify CompoundIQ within three (3) business days if a license is suspended, restricted, revoked or not renewed.\n- You represent that neither your practice nor any Provider is excluded, debarred or suspended from any federal or state health care program, and you will notify CompoundIQ within three (3) business days if that changes.\n- Every prescription must be issued by a Provider in the course of a valid practitioner-patient relationship, for a legitimate medical purpose, in compliance with Applicable Law. Your Providers are solely responsible for diagnosis, treatment decisions, dosing, monitoring and follow-up.\n- Compounded medications are not FDA-approved. Your Providers are responsible for deciding that a compounded medication is clinically appropriate for each patient, including, where Applicable Law requires, documenting the clinical difference from a commercially available product. Showing an ingredient or formulation on the Platform is not a representation that it is appropriate or lawful for a particular patient.\n- The Platform does not transmit prescriptions for controlled substances.\n\n## 5. Patients and consent\n\n- You will add patients and their information accurately, or invite patients to complete their own details through the Platform.\n- You are responsible for obtaining any consents Applicable Law requires, including consent to receive text messages, before you or the Platform contacts a patient. The Platform records consent where a patient gives it and honors STOP requests.\n- You will give patients your Notice of Privacy Practices as HIPAA requires and will answer patient questions about their treatment.\n\n## 6. Orders, pricing and payment\n\n- You set the price the patient pays for each Order, which must be shown to the patient before payment. The Pharmacy's wholesale price and shipping charge are shown to you on the Platform.\n- CompoundIQ collects payment from the patient through its payment processor, pays the Pharmacy its wholesale price and shipping, and pays your clinic the remainder, less any platform fee you have agreed to in writing, through the payment processor's payout schedule.\n- You are responsible for making sure that your pricing, and any amount your clinic receives from an Order, complies with Applicable Law, including laws on fee-splitting, patient brokering and the sale of medications by practitioners in your state.\n- Orders paid through the Platform are cash-pay. You will not bill any insurer or federal health care program for an Order paid through the Platform.\n- Refunds, cancellations and chargebacks are handled as described on the Platform and in the Patient Terms of Use. Amounts refunded to a patient because of your error are deducted from later payouts.\n\n## 7. No payment for referrals\n\n- Nothing in these Terms is, or will be treated as, an offer or payment of anything of value in return for referring patients, prescriptions or business, and no amount paid under these Terms is based on the volume or value of referrals to CompoundIQ or any Pharmacy.\n- You will not accept anything of value from a Pharmacy in return for sending Orders to it.\n\n## 8. Privacy and security\n\n- CompoundIQ handles PHI as your business associate under the BAA. If these Terms conflict with the BAA on PHI, the BAA controls.\n- You will use and disclose only the minimum necessary PHI through the Platform and will not enter PHI the Platform does not need.\n\n## 9. Acceptable use\n\n- You will use the Platform only for your practice's lawful prescribing and ordering. You will not attempt to access another clinic's data, probe or test the Platform's security without written permission, interfere with its operation, copy or resell it, or use it to send marketing messages to patients.\n\n## 10. Fees\n\n- CompoundIQ charges no fee under these Terms unless you agree to one in a separate written order form. Any fee will be fixed in advance, will not be based on the volume or value of Orders or referrals, and will apply only after you accept it.\n\n## 11. Availability and changes to the Platform\n\n- CompoundIQ works to keep the Platform available and secure but does not guarantee uninterrupted service. You will keep a way to issue prescriptions if the Platform is unavailable.\n- CompoundIQ may change or improve the Platform. We will give reasonable notice of changes that materially reduce its functionality.\n\n## 12. Records and cooperation\n\n- You will keep the medical records Applicable Law requires for your patients. CompoundIQ keeps Order, prescription and payment records as described in the BAA and its Privacy Policy.\n- Each party will cooperate reasonably with the other, and with any board, regulator or law enforcement inquiry about an Order, to the extent Applicable Law allows.\n\n## 13. Suspension and termination\n\n- These Terms continue until terminated. Either party may terminate on thirty (30) days' written notice.\n- CompoundIQ may suspend your access, in whole or for a Provider, immediately if a required license lapses or is restricted, a Provider or your practice becomes excluded from a health care program, there is a credible patient safety or fraud concern, or you breach the BAA or section 9. CompoundIQ will tell you the reason and how to resolve it.\n- Either party may terminate immediately for a material breach not cured within fifteen (15) days of written notice, or if the other party becomes insolvent.\n- On termination, Orders already paid will be completed or cancelled and refunded, CompoundIQ will pay amounts owed to you, and PHI will be returned or protected as the BAA requires. Sections 4, 6, 7, 8, 12, 14, 15, 16 and 17 survive.\n\n## 14. Confidentiality\n\n- Each party will protect the other's non-public business information with at least reasonable care and use it only to perform these Terms. This does not apply to information that is public, already known, independently developed or lawfully received from someone else, or that must be disclosed by law.\n\n## 15. Indemnification\n\n- You will defend and indemnify CompoundIQ against third-party claims arising from your Providers' prescribing, diagnosis or treatment, your failure to hold required licenses or consents, your pricing, your breach of these Terms or the BAA, or your violation of Applicable Law.\n- CompoundIQ will defend and indemnify you against third-party claims arising from a failure of the Platform to transmit an Order as signed, CompoundIQ's breach of these Terms or the BAA, or CompoundIQ's violation of Applicable Law.\n- The indemnified party must give prompt notice, reasonable cooperation and control of the defense, and no settlement may admit fault for the other party without its written consent.\n\n## 16. Disclaimers and limitation of liability\n\n- Except as stated in these Terms, the Platform is provided as available and CompoundIQ makes no other warranties, including warranties of merchantability or fitness for a particular purpose.\n- Except for indemnification obligations, breach of confidentiality or the BAA, and gross negligence or willful misconduct, neither party is liable for indirect, incidental, special, consequential or punitive damages, or lost profits, and each party's total liability under these Terms is limited to the greater of the fees you paid to CompoundIQ in the twelve (12) months before the claim or five thousand dollars ($5,000).\n\n## 17. General\n\n- Changes to these Terms. CompoundIQ may update these Terms with at least thirty (30) days' notice in the Platform. Material changes require your acceptance before they apply to you; if you do not accept, you may terminate.\n- Relationship. The parties are independent contractors. Nothing in these Terms creates a partnership, joint venture, agency or employment relationship.\n- Assignment. Neither party may assign these Terms without the other's consent, except to a successor in a merger or sale of substantially all of its business that assumes these Terms in writing.\n- Force majeure. Neither party is liable for delay caused by events beyond its reasonable control, if it gives prompt notice and resumes performance as soon as practicable.\n- Compliance with law. These Terms will be interpreted to comply with Applicable Law. If a provision is found unlawful, the parties will amend it to the minimum extent necessary and the rest remains in effect.\n- Governing law and disputes. These Terms are governed by the laws of the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania, without regard to its conflict of laws rules. The parties will first try in good faith to resolve any dispute through discussion between senior representatives for thirty (30) days. If not resolved, either party may bring the dispute exclusively in the state or federal courts located in Allegheny County, Pennsylvania, and each party consents to that jurisdiction. Either party may seek urgent injunctive relief in any court of competent jurisdiction.\n- Electronic acceptance. Acceptance in the CompoundIQ portal by an authorized representative of the clinic, recorded with name, title, account, date and time and the version and text of these Terms, is a signature by the clinic.\n"

/** src/content/legal/pharmacy-terms-v1.0.md (sha256 efedb4384f2b09a479b55cbb0e7aa178790f1228ba77ec460a9a7657ba9b89ef) */
export const PHARMACY_TERMS_V1_0_TEXT = "# CompoundIQ Pharmacy Terms of Service\n\n_Version 1.0. Effective October 12, 2026._\n\nThese Pharmacy Terms of Service (the \"Terms\") are an agreement between CompoundIQ (\"CompoundIQ\", \"we\") and the pharmacy that accepts them in the CompoundIQ onboarding portal (\"Pharmacy\", \"you\"). They take effect when CompoundIQ approves your pharmacy in the portal (the \"Effective Date\"). The Business Associate Agreement or data-sharing agreement you accept in the portal (the \"Data Agreement\") is part of these Terms.\n\n## 1. Definitions\n\n- \"Platform\" means CompoundIQ's software, portals, APIs, order routing, payment collection and related services.\n- \"Clinic\" means a medical practice that uses the Platform. \"Prescriber\" means a licensed practitioner at a Clinic who signs prescriptions on the Platform.\n- \"Order\" means a signed, patient-specific prescription for a compounded or other medication, with the patient, shipping and payment information needed to fill it, sent to you through the Platform.\n- \"Catalog\" means the formulations, strengths, dosage forms and wholesale prices you list on the Platform.\n- \"Applicable Law\" means all federal, state and local laws, regulations and board rules that apply to your activities, including pharmacy practice acts, the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act (including sections 503A and 503B), the Controlled Substances Act, USP standards adopted by your state, HIPAA, the federal Anti-Kickback Statute and state anti-kickback, fee-splitting and patient-brokering laws.\n- \"PHI\" has the meaning given in HIPAA.\n\n## 2. What CompoundIQ does and does not do\n\n- CompoundIQ provides technology that lets Prescribers create and sign prescriptions, routes each Order to the pharmacy the Prescriber selects, collects payment from the patient, and tracks the Order to delivery.\n- CompoundIQ is not a pharmacy, prescriber, health care provider, pharmacy benefit manager, insurer or payer. CompoundIQ does not practice pharmacy or medicine and does not direct or control your professional judgment.\n- Prescribers and patients choose the pharmacy. CompoundIQ does not guarantee you any volume of Orders, and you are not required to accept any minimum number of Orders. These Terms are non-exclusive for both parties.\n\n## 3. Eligibility, licensure and credentials\n\n- You must hold, and keep current, every license, permit and registration Applicable Law requires to compound and dispense the products in your Catalog and to ship them to each state you serve, including non-resident pharmacy licenses where required.\n- You must provide accurate details in onboarding, including legal name, NCPDP ID, NPI, facility type (503A or 503B), each state license with its document, and whether each license covers sterile compounding. CompoundIQ verifies each license before your pharmacy is shown to Prescribers and may ask for proof at any time and at least once a year.\n- You must notify CompoundIQ within three (3) business days if any license, permit, registration or accreditation is suspended, restricted, revoked, placed on probation or not renewed, or if you become the subject of a board or FDA enforcement action. CompoundIQ will stop routing Orders to you for the affected state or product.\n- You represent that neither you nor any pharmacist or owner is excluded, debarred or suspended from any federal or state health care program, and you will notify CompoundIQ within three (3) business days if that changes.\n- The Platform does not transmit prescriptions for controlled substances. A DEA number you provide is recorded for identification only.\n\n## 4. Professional responsibility and Order handling\n\n- You are solely responsible for the practice of pharmacy, including prescription verification, drug utilization review, compounding, beyond-use dating, labeling, packaging, dispensing, patient counseling, adverse event reporting and recalls.\n- You will exercise independent professional judgment on every Order. You may decline or cancel any Order you believe is invalid, unsafe, outside your capabilities or not permitted by Applicable Law, and you will give the reason using the Platform's rejection codes.\n- If you are a 503A pharmacy, you will compound only for identified individual patients on valid patient-specific prescriptions, will not compound drugs that are essentially copies of commercially available drug products except as Applicable Law permits, and will use bulk drug substances only as section 503A allows. If you are a 503B outsourcing facility, you will meet all section 503B and current good manufacturing practice requirements.\n- Some ingredients are shown on the Platform with a compounding status, such as \"pending FDA evaluation\". Showing or allowing an ingredient is not a representation by CompoundIQ that it may lawfully be compounded. You are responsible for confirming that you can lawfully compound every product you accept.\n- You will compound and dispense in compliance with the USP chapters your state has adopted, including chapters 795, 797 and 800 as applicable, and will keep your Catalog limited to products you are able to prepare under those standards.\n\n## 5. How Orders reach you and service levels\n\n- You choose how Orders reach you: through your API, through your web portal, or by fax. You must keep the connection details current. Credentials you give CompoundIQ are stored encrypted and are used only to deliver Orders to you.\n- You will acknowledge each Order within eight (8) business hours, update its status on the Platform (accepted, compounding, shipped with tracking, or rejected), and ship accepted Orders within your stated turnaround. Orders received after your daily cutoff may ship the next business day.\n- If you do not acknowledge an Order within the service level, CompoundIQ may alert you, escalate, and with the Prescriber's direction reroute the Order to another pharmacy. Repeated service failures are grounds for suspension under section 14.\n\n## 6. Shipping\n\n- You will ship only to states where you are licensed to dispense, using the carriers and cold-chain methods you list, in tamper-evident packaging that protects product integrity and patient privacy.\n- You bear the risk of loss, damage and temperature excursion until delivery, and you will replace or refund lost or compromised shipments as your policies and Applicable Law require.\n\n## 7. Catalog and pricing\n\n- You will keep your Catalog accurate and complete, including formulation, strength, dosage form, quantity, wholesale price and availability. You will give at least thirty (30) days' notice of price increases through the Platform. Orders placed before the change are filled at the price shown when the Order was signed.\n- Your prices must be the same for every Clinic on the Platform and must not vary based on the volume or value of Orders a Clinic or Prescriber sends you. You are responsible for any usual-and-customary pricing obligations you have to third-party payers.\n\n## 8. Payments and payouts\n\n- CompoundIQ collects payment from patients through its payment processor. For each paid Order you fill, CompoundIQ will pay you the wholesale price in your Catalog at the time the Order was signed, plus the shipping charge you listed, through CompoundIQ's payment processor on its standard payout schedule, normally within seven (7) days after the Order ships, with a remittance report listing each Order paid.\n- Amounts for Orders that are cancelled before you ship, rejected by you, or refunded to the patient because of your error or failure to ship are not payable, or are deducted from later payouts. Chargebacks caused by your failure to fill or deliver may be deducted in the same way.\n- You will not bill any insurer, federal health care program or patient for an Order paid through the Platform, unless CompoundIQ and you agree otherwise in writing.\n- You pay no fee to CompoundIQ to receive Orders. Any future fee will be a fixed, fair-market-value fee for defined services, set in advance in writing, not based on the volume or value of Orders, and will apply only after you accept updated Terms.\n\n## 9. No payment for referrals\n\n- Nothing in these Terms is, or will be treated as, an offer or payment of anything of value in return for referring patients, prescriptions or business. No amount paid under these Terms is based on the volume or value of referrals.\n- You will not offer or pay anything of value to CompoundIQ, a Clinic, a Prescriber or a patient to induce Orders, and you will not ask Prescribers or patients to send prescriptions to you outside the Platform in a way that bypasses a patient's choice of pharmacy.\n\n## 10. Privacy and security\n\n- You and CompoundIQ will handle PHI as required by HIPAA, state privacy law and the Data Agreement, and will use and disclose only the minimum necessary to fill and support each Order.\n- Each person who uses your account must have their own login and use two-step sign-in. You are responsible for activity under your accounts and will tell CompoundIQ promptly if you suspect unauthorized access.\n- You will maintain a written information security program appropriate to the PHI you handle, and will notify CompoundIQ of any security incident affecting Platform data as the Data Agreement requires.\n\n## 11. Records, audits and cooperation\n\n- You will keep prescription, compounding, dispensing and shipping records for each Order for the period Applicable Law requires, and at least six (6) years.\n- On reasonable notice, you will give CompoundIQ the records needed to confirm your licensure and compliance with these Terms, and will cooperate with any Clinic, board, FDA or law enforcement inquiry about an Order, to the extent Applicable Law allows.\n\n## 12. Insurance\n\n- You will maintain, with reputable insurers: pharmacist and pharmacy professional liability insurance of at least $1,000,000 per claim and $3,000,000 in the aggregate; general liability insurance of at least $1,000,000 per occurrence and $2,000,000 in the aggregate; and cyber liability insurance of at least $1,000,000. You will provide certificates on request and notify CompoundIQ within ten (10) days of cancellation or material reduction.\n\n## 13. Names, marks and marketing\n\n- CompoundIQ may display your pharmacy name, location, licensed states, Catalog and service details to Prescribers on the Platform. Neither party will otherwise use the other's name or marks in advertising without prior written consent.\n- Products are dispensed and labeled under your pharmacy name. The Platform does not offer white-labeled dispensing.\n\n## 14. Suspension and termination\n\n- These Terms continue until terminated. Either party may terminate on thirty (30) days' written notice.\n- CompoundIQ may suspend Order routing to you immediately, in whole or for a state or product, if a license lapses or is restricted, you become excluded from a health care program, there is a credible patient safety concern, a recall affects your products, you breach the Data Agreement, or you repeatedly miss service levels. CompoundIQ will tell you the reason and how to resolve it.\n- Either party may terminate immediately for a material breach not cured within fifteen (15) days of written notice, or if the other party becomes insolvent.\n- On termination, you will complete or properly cancel and refund Orders already accepted, CompoundIQ will pay amounts owed for Orders you filled, and each party will return or destroy the other's confidential information as the Data Agreement and Applicable Law allow. Sections 4, 8, 10, 11, 15, 16, 17 and 18 survive.\n\n## 15. Confidentiality\n\n- Each party will protect the other's non-public business information with at least reasonable care and use it only to perform these Terms. This does not apply to information that is public, already known, independently developed or lawfully received from someone else, or that must be disclosed by law.\n\n## 16. Indemnification\n\n- You will defend and indemnify CompoundIQ against third-party claims arising from your compounding, dispensing, labeling, shipping or counseling, your failure to hold required licenses, your breach of these Terms or the Data Agreement, or your violation of Applicable Law.\n- CompoundIQ will defend and indemnify you against third-party claims arising from a failure of the Platform to transmit an Order as signed, CompoundIQ's breach of these Terms or the Data Agreement, or CompoundIQ's violation of Applicable Law.\n- The indemnified party must give prompt notice, reasonable cooperation and control of the defense, and no settlement may admit fault for the other party without its written consent.\n\n## 17. Disclaimers and limitation of liability\n\n- The Platform is provided as available. CompoundIQ does not warrant that it will be uninterrupted or error free, and you will keep your own process for receiving prescriptions if the Platform is unavailable.\n- Except for indemnification obligations, breach of confidentiality or the Data Agreement, and gross negligence or willful misconduct, neither party is liable for indirect, incidental, special, consequential or punitive damages, or lost profits, and each party's total liability under these Terms is limited to the amounts paid or payable to you under these Terms in the twelve (12) months before the claim.\n\n## 18. General\n\n- Changes. CompoundIQ may update these Terms with at least thirty (30) days' notice in the Platform. Material changes require your acceptance before they apply to you; if you do not accept, you may terminate.\n- Relationship. The parties are independent contractors. Nothing in these Terms creates a partnership, joint venture, agency or employment relationship.\n- Assignment. Neither party may assign these Terms without the other's consent, except to a successor in a merger or sale of substantially all of its business that assumes these Terms in writing.\n- Force majeure. Neither party is liable for delay caused by events beyond its reasonable control, if it gives prompt notice and resumes performance as soon as practicable. This does not excuse your obligation to protect patient safety or to cancel and refund Orders you cannot fill.\n- Compliance with law. These Terms will be interpreted to comply with Applicable Law. If a provision is found unlawful, the parties will amend it to the minimum extent necessary and the rest remains in effect.\n- Order of precedence. If these Terms conflict with the Data Agreement on PHI, the Data Agreement controls. A signed written agreement between the parties controls over these Terms where it says so expressly.\n- Governing law and disputes. These Terms are governed by the laws of the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania, without regard to its conflict of laws rules. The parties will first try in good faith to resolve any dispute through discussion between senior representatives for thirty (30) days. If not resolved, either party may bring the dispute exclusively in the state or federal courts located in Allegheny County, Pennsylvania, and each party consents to that jurisdiction. Either party may seek urgent injunctive relief in any court of competent jurisdiction.\n- Electronic acceptance. Acceptance in the CompoundIQ portal by an authorized representative of the pharmacy, recorded with name, title, account, date and time and the version and text of these Terms, is a signature by the pharmacy.\n"

